{"item_id": "atlas_test_00114", "reference_source": "atlas_reasoning", "product": "traffic signal parts from China, specifically an external housing/shell made from Lexan plastic and a cast aluminum bracket for attaching a traffic light", "cbp_reference": "Reasoning summarized from the CBP ruling (gold code 8530.90.0000):\nThe product in question consists of two components: the external housing/shell for a traffic signal and a cast aluminum bracket. The molded housing is constructed from 100% Lexan plastic and is designed to meet Federal Highway Administration standards for housing LED traffic signal components. Importantly, at the time of import, this housing does not incorporate any electronic components, which indicates that it is a structural part rather than a complete signaling device. \n\nThe cast aluminum bracket serves a specific function in the assembly of the traffic signal, allowing for attachment to an overhead guy-wire and facilitating the passage of electrical cables. This bracket is also specifically manufactured for use with traffic signals, further solidifying its classification as a part of traffic control equipment.\n\nBoth items are classified under HTS subheading 8530.90.0000, which covers electrical signaling, safety, or traffic control equipment and parts thereof. The ruling confirms that these products fall under this classification, which is appropriate given their intended use and function in traffic signaling systems. Additionally, the ruling notes that products classified under this subheading are subject to an additional 25% ad valorem duty due to tariffs imposed on certain products from China, which is relevant for importation purposes.", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "8530.90.00.00", "deciding_gri": "GRI 1", "gri_path": ["GRI 1: heading 8530 covers electrical signaling, safety or traffic control equipment for roads and parts thereof", "Section XVI note 2(b): the housing is not itself a good of a specific heading of ch. 84/85 and is suitable solely or principally for traffic signals, so it goes with them in 8530.90", "Section XVI note 1(g)/Section XV note 2 checked: the housing is not a part of general use", "GRI 6: 8530.90 Parts; single statistical line 8530.90.00.00"], "rationale": "The Lexan external housing is identifiable as a part of a traffic signal, which is road traffic control equipment of heading 8530. Under Section XVI note 2(b), a part suitable solely or principally for that apparatus is classified with it, in 8530.90.00.00. NY N359472 (in force) classified a housing for a roadway pedestrian warning light in 8530.90.0000. The first-named article (the housing) decides; the bracket should be classified separately, most likely in the same line.", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "3926.90.99.89", "reason": "plastic articles n.e.s.o.i.; the housing is a dedicated part of traffic signaling equipment and goes with it under Section XVI note 2(b)"}, {"code": "8512.90", "reason": "covers parts of signaling equipment for cycles and motor vehicles, not roadside traffic signals"}, {"code": "7616.99.51", "reason": "aluminum articles; would only apply to the bracket if it were a general-purpose mounting not dedicated to traffic signals (classify separately)"}]}}
{"item_id": "atlas_test_00117", "reference_source": "ruling:N323764", "product": "flagging tape made of PVC with plasticizers", "cbp_reference": "CBP ruling N323764, gold code 3920.43.5000:\nkory Gln Grimes, Iowa 50111 RE: The tariff classification of polyvinyl chloride (PVC) strip from China Dear Mr. Engle: In your letter dated January 19, 2022, you requested a tariff classification ruling. The product under consideration is referred to as flagging tape. You indicate that the product is constructed of PVC containing 8 percent plasticizers. It is in the form of a non-adhesive strip that will be imported in a roll. The strip measures 1.5 inches wide and comes in various lengths from 100 to 1000 feet. The tape will be tied around tree branches or wooden stakes for marking purposes. The applicable subheading for the PVC strip flagging tape will be 3920.43.5000, Harmonized Tariff Schedule of the United States (HTSUS), which provides for other plates, sheets, film, foil and strip, of plastics, noncellular and not reinforced, laminated, supported or similarly combined with other materials: of polymers of vinyl chloride: containing by weight not less than 6 percent of plasticizers: other. The general rate of duty will be 4.2 percent ad valorem. Pursuant to U.S. Note 20 to Subchapter III, Chapter 99, HTSUS, products of China classified under subheading 3920.43.5000, HTSUS, unless specifically excluded, are subject to an additional 25 percent ad valorem rate of duty. At the time of importation, you must report the Chapter 99 subheading, i.e., 9903.88.02, in addition to subheading 3920.43.5000, HTSUS, listed above. The HTSUS is subject to periodic amendment, so you should exercise reasonable care in monitoring the status of goods covered by the Note cited above and the applicable Chapter 99 subheading. For background information regarding the trade remedy initiated pursuant to Section 301 of the Trade Act of 1974, including information on exclusions and their effective dates, you may refer to the relevant parts of the USTR and CBP websites, which are available at https://ustr.gov/issue-areas/enforcement/section-301-investigations/tariff-actions and https://www.cbp.gov/trade/remedies/301-certain-products-china, respectively. Duty rates are provided for your convenience and are subject to change. The text of the most recent HTSUS and the accompanying duty rates are provided on the World Wide Web at https://hts.usitc.gov/current. This ruling is being issued under the provisions of Part 177 of the Customs Regulations (19 C.F.R. 177). A copy of the ruling or the control number indicated above should be provided with the entry documents filed at the time this merchandise is imported. If you have any questions regarding the ruling, contact National Import Specialist Christina Allen at julie.c.allen@cbp.dhs.gov. Sincerely, Steven A. Mack Director National Commodity Specialist Division", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "3920.43.50.00", "deciding_gri": "GRI 1", "gri_path": ["GRI 1: heading 3920 covers other plates, sheets, film, foil and strip of plastics, noncellular and not reinforced; flagging tape is a plain PVC strip", "Heading 3919 excluded because the tape is not self-adhesive; chapter 49 not applicable as the tape is not printed", "GRI 6: 3920.43 PVC containing not less than 6% plasticizers; 3920.43.50 other (not imitation patent leather)"], "rationale": "Flagging tape is a non-adhesive, noncellular, unreinforced PVC strip in rolls. That falls in heading 3920 rather than 3919 (self-adhesive) or 3926. Being plasticized PVC, it falls in 3920.43, and 3920.43.50.00 as not being imitation patent leather. NY N332349 (in force) classified a non-adhesive PVC tape in 3920.43.5000.", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "3919.10.20", "reason": "flagging tape has no adhesive, so it is not a self-adhesive tape"}, {"code": "3920.49.00.00", "reason": "applies to PVC with less than 6% plasticizers; the tape is plasticized (flexible)"}, {"code": "4911.99.80", "reason": "only for printed barricade/warning tape where the printing is not incidental; plain flagging tape is unprinted"}, {"code": "3926.90.99", "reason": "strip in rolls that is only cut to width is not worked beyond heading 3920"}]}}
{"item_id": "atlas_test_00121", "reference_source": "ruling:N265787", "product": "a stainless steel broiler pan with a glass tray used for broiling food in an oven", "cbp_reference": "CBP ruling N265787, gold code 7323.93.0045:\n is made of stainless steel. It is designed to be used as a roasting pan in the oven. It is round in shape and features two handles, one on each of two opposing sides. The pan measures approximately 12” in diameter x 1¼” in depth. Wire rack – This item is made of stainless steel wire and fits on top of the broiler pan. It is round in shape and measures approximately 12½” in diameter. Wire Cooking Tongs – This item is designed to be used to turn food cooked on the broiler pan and on outdoor grills with wire racks. The tongs measure approximately 16” in length by 2” in width at the widest part. The applicable subheading for the Broiler Pan and the Wire Rack will be 7323.93.0045, Harmonized Tariff Schedule of the United States (HTSUS), which provides for kitchen…articles…of…steel…: other: of stainless steel…cooking and kitchen ware: other: cooking ware: other. The rate of duty will be 2 percent ad valorem. The applicable subheading for the Wire Cooking Tongs will be 8215.99.5000, Harmonized Tariff Schedule of the United States (HTSUS), which provides for spoons, forks, ladles, skimmers, cake-servers, fish-knives, butter-knives, sugar tongs and similar kitchen or tableware; and base metal parts thereof: other: other: other (including parts). The rate of duty will be 5.3 percent ad valorem. Articles classifiable under subheading 7323.93.0045, HTS, and 8215.99.5000, HTS, which are products of India, may be entitled to duty free treatment under the Generalized System of Preferences (GSP) upon compliance with all applicable regulations. The GSP is subject to modification and periodic suspension, which may affect the status of your transaction at the time of entry for consumption or withdrawal from warehouse. To obtain current information on GSP, check our Web site at www.cbp.gov and search for the term \"GSP.\" You have also asked if the country of origin marking that has been proposed is acceptable. Marked samples were not submitted with your letter for review. You have stated that the broiler pan will be marked with the words “Made in India” etched on the bottom of the pan. However, due to the structure of the wire rack and wire cooking tongs, you have stated that it is impossible to mark those items and instead, each of those items will be imported in a polybag with a sticker on which is printed the words “Made in India.” The marking statute, section 304, Tariff Act of 1930, as amended (19 U.S.C. 1304), provides that, unless excepted, every article of foreign origin (or its container) imported into the U.S. shall be marked in a conspicuous place as legibly, indelibly and permanently as the nature of the article (or its container) will permit, in such a manner as to indicate to the ultimate purchaser in the U.S. the English name of the country of origin of the article. As provided in section 134.41(b), Customs Regulations (19 CFR 134.41(b)), the country of origin marking is considered conspicuous if the ultimate purchaser in the U.S. is able to find the marking easily and read it without strain. Section 134.1(d), defines the ultimate purchaser as generally the last person in the U.S. who will receive the article in the form in which it was imported. If an imported article is to be sold at retail in its imported form, the purchaser at retail is the ultimate purchaser. In this case, the ultimate purchasers of the broiler pans, wire racks and wire cooking tongs are the consumers that purchase the item at the retail level. With regard to the perman", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "7323.93.00.35", "deciding_gri": "GRI 3(b)", "gri_path": ["GRI 1: stainless steel component falls in heading 7323 (household kitchen articles of steel); glass tray alone would fall in 7013 (glassware for kitchen use); neither heading covers the whole", "GRI 3(a): each heading describes only part of the composite good, so they are equally specific", "GRI 3(b): the stainless steel broiler pan gives the essential character (named article, role in broiling, bulk); the glass tray is ancillary", "GRI 6: 7323.93 of stainless steel; cooking ware; suffix .35 bakeware (cookware not suitable for stove top use), since a broiler pan is used in the oven"], "rationale": "The broiler pan combines a stainless steel pan with a glass tray. Headings 7323 and 7013 each cover only one of the materials, so GRI 3(b) applies, and the stainless steel pan, the named article that holds the food for broiling, gives the essential character. Stainless steel cookware used only in the oven is bakeware under 7323.93.00.35. NY K85319 (in force) supports classifying stainless steel cookware accessories in 7323.93, though its goods (barbecue accessories) differ.", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "7013.49.10", "reason": "glass kitchenware; the glass tray does not give the essential character of the broiler pan"}, {"code": "7323.93.00.45", "reason": "other cooking ware suitable for stove top use; a broiler pan is oven-only"}, {"code": "7323.93.00.60", "reason": "kitchen ware other than cooking ware; the broiler pan is used to cook food"}, {"code": "8516.90", "reason": "not a part of an electric oven; it is a free-standing cookware article"}]}}
{"item_id": "atlas_test_00123", "reference_source": "ruling:R04660", "product": "a power tool kit that includes a rechargeable drill, worklight, charger, battery, drill bits, and nylon storage bag, packaged for retail sale", "cbp_reference": "CBP ruling R04660, gold code 8467.21.0010:\nvity (i.e., working wood and similar materials). Finally, the articles are put up in a manner suitable for sale directly to users without repacking. Therefore, the set in question is within the term \"goods put up in sets for retail sale.\" GRI 3(b) states in part that goods put up in sets for retail sale, which cannot be classified by reference to GRI 3(a), are to be classified as if they consisted of the component which gives them their essential character. In your letter, you state that you believe the essential character of the retail set is imparted by the drill/driver. This office agrees. The applicable subheading for the 18 volt cordless drill/driver with 18 volt flashlight set, model 33629, will be 8467.21.0010, Harmonized Tariff Schedule of the United States (HTSUS), which provides for tools for working in the hand with self-contained electric motor: drills of all kinds…rotary: battery powered. The rate of duty will be 1.7 percent. Duty rates are provided for your convenience and are subject to change. The text of the most recent HTSUS and the accompanying duty rates are provided on World Wide Web at http://www.usitc.gov/tata/hts/. This ruling is being issued under the provisions of Part 177 of the Customs Regulations (19 CFR 177). A copy of the ruling or the control number indicated above should be provided with the entry documents filed at the time this merchandise is imported. If you have any questions regarding the ruling, contact National Import Specialist Patricia O’Donnell at 646-733-3011. Sincerely, Robert B. Swierupski Director, National Commodity Specialist Division", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "8467.21.00.10", "deciding_gri": "GRI 3(b)", "gri_path": ["GRI 1: components fall in different headings (drill 8467, worklight 8513, charger 8504, battery 8507, bits 8207, bag 4202); no single heading covers the whole", "GRI 3(a): each heading covers only part of the set, so they are equally specific", "GRI 3(b): retail set put up for a specific activity (drilling) and sold without repacking; the rechargeable drill gives the essential character by its role in use; battery, charger, bits and bag serve the drill and the worklight is ancillary", "GRI 6: 8467.21 drills of all kinds; rotary; battery powered -> 8467.21.00.10"], "rationale": "The kit is a retail set of articles from several headings, put up together for drilling. Under GRI 3(b) the rechargeable drill gives the essential character: the battery, charger, bits and bag support it, and the worklight is incidental. The set is therefore classified as a battery-powered rotary drill in 8467.21.00.10. CBP applied GRI 3(b) to cordless tool kits with chargers and accessories in HQ H86275 and NY F86724 (both in force).", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "8513.10", "reason": "the worklight is a secondary item and does not give the set its essential character"}, {"code": "8507.60", "reason": "the battery only powers the tools; the drill performs the activity the set is sold for"}, {"code": "8504.40.95", "reason": "the charger is an accessory to the battery, not the essential character"}, {"code": "8467.21.00.70", "reason": "applies to drills that are not rotary battery powered, including hammer drills; this is a rechargeable rotary drill"}]}}
{"item_id": "atlas_test_00125", "reference_source": "atlas_reasoning", "product": "metal recliner mechanisms", "cbp_reference": "Reasoning summarized from the CBP ruling (gold code 9401.90.5080):\nThe product in question consists of metal recliner mechanisms, specifically the Handle Release 17 Mechanism, which includes various components such as the Base Metal Rocker, Cross Tube, Drive Tube, Reinforcing Tube, Drive Spring, Recliner Mechanisms (LAF and RAF), and Lock Spring. According to the ruling NY M85637, these components are classified under HTSUS 9401.90.5080, which covers \"Seats (other than those of heading 9402), whether or not convertible into beds, and parts thereof: Parts: Other: Other, Other.\" This classification is appropriate because the recliner mechanisms are integral parts of seating furniture. The ruling confirms that even when imported separately, the components maintain their classification under this HTS code, as they are specifically designed for use in recliner seating. The duty rate for this classification is free, further supporting its suitability for the described product.", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "9401.99.90.85", "deciding_gri": "GRI 1", "gri_path": ["GRI 1: heading 9401 covers seats and parts thereof; the mechanism is identifiable as a part of recliner seats", "Chapter 94 note 1 and Section XV note 2 checked: a recliner mechanism is a dedicated seat part, not a part of general use (screws, springs, hinges)", "GRI 6: 9401.99 parts other than of wood; 9401.99.90 other (not motor vehicle, cane, or rubber/plastics); suffix .85 other"], "rationale": "A metal recliner mechanism is made to fit and operate recliner seats, so heading 9401 covers it as a part of seats. It is not a part of general use of base metal. Because it is not wood, it falls in 9401.99 and in 9401.99.90.85 as other parts. NY N012776 (in force) classified a metal recliner mechanism as a seat part in 9401.90.5080, the predecessor line of today's 9401.99.90.", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "9401.91", "reason": "parts of wood; the mechanism is metal"}, {"code": "9401.99.10", "reason": "limited to parts of motor vehicle seats; recliner mechanisms are for household furniture"}, {"code": "8302.42", "reason": "base metal furniture mountings/fittings; the recliner mechanism is a dedicated functional seat part, as CBP held in N012776"}, {"code": "9401.99.90.30", "reason": "covers sofa bed seat deck components, not recliner mechanisms"}]}}
{"item_id": "atlas_test_00128", "reference_source": "ruling:N328038", "product": "shower grates used in residential showers for draining water", "cbp_reference": "CBP ruling N328038, gold code 7419.80.5010:\nitem numbers 69125 and 59614 are composite articles comprised of more than one metal. Regarding the classification of composite articles, Section XV, Note 7 of the HTSUS states that “articles of base metal containing two or more base metals are to be treated as articles of the base metal predominating by weight over each of the other metals”. Based on the information provided to our office, the metal in these shower grates that predominates by weight is brass. Therefore, IPS item numbers 69125 and 59614 will be classified under heading 7419, HTSUS, which provides for other articles of copper. The applicable subheading for IPS item number 69693 (Model No. ABA7101VB), IPS item number 69125 (Model No. ABA7137ORB), IPS item number 59614 (Model No. ABA5149ORB), and IPS item number 59525 (Model No. ABAB7101SN) will be 7419.80.5010, HTSUS, which provides for other articles of copper, other…brass plumbing goods not elsewhere specified or included. The rate of duty will be free. The applicable subheading for IPS item number 63643 (Model No. ABA44010MB) will be 7326.90.8688, HTSUS, which provides for other articles of iron or steel, other…other. The rate of duty will be 2.9 percent ad valorem. Duty rates are provided for your convenience and are subject to change. The text of the most recent HTSUS and the accompanying duty rates are provided at https://hts.usitc.gov/current. Pursuant to U.S. Note 20 to Subchapter III, Chapter 99, HTSUS, products of China classified under subheadings 7419.80.5010 and 7326.90.8688, HTSUS, unless specifically excluded, are subject to an additional 25 percent ad valorem rate of duty. At the time of importation, you must report the Chapter 99 subheading, i.e., 9903.88.03, in addition to subheading 7419.80.5010 and 7326.90.8688, HTSUS, listed above. The HTSUS is subject to periodic amendment so you should exercise reasonable care in monitoring the status of goods covered by the Note cited above and the applicable Chapter 99 subheading. For background information regarding the trade remedy initiated pursuant to Section 301 of the Trade Act of 1974, you may refer to the relevant parts of the USTR and CBP websites, which are available at https://ustr.gov/issue-areas/enforcement/section-301-investigations/tariff-actions and https://www.cbp.gov/trade/remedies/301-certain-products-china, respectively. This ruling is being issued under the provisions of Part 177 of the Customs Regulations (19 C.F.R. 177). A copy of the ruling or the control number indicated above should be provided with the entry documents filed at the time this merchandise is imported. If you have any questions regarding the ruling, contact National Import Specialist Ann Taub at Ann.Taub@cbp.dhs.gov. Sincerely, Steven A. Mack Director National Commodity Specialist Division", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "7419.80.50.10", "deciding_gri": "GRI 1", "gri_path": ["GRI 1: the grate is an article of base metal classified by its material; no chapter 84 heading applies (not a valve or tap), and heading 7324 sanitary ware covers articles such as sinks and basins, not drain grates", "Assumed brass: heading 7419 other articles of copper; cast grates that are machined are advanced beyond 7419.20 'cast, not further worked' (per N327820)", "GRI 6: 7419.80.50 other; statistical suffix .10 brass plumbing goods n.e.s.o.i."], "rationale": "Shower grates are classified by their constituent material, and the description does not state it. NY N327820 (in force) classified a cast and machined brass (nickel bronze) shower grate used to drain wastewater in 7419.80.5010 as a brass plumbing good. That is the best guess here, but a stainless steel grate would fall in 7326.90.86.88 and a plastic one in 3922.90.00, so the material must be confirmed.", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "7326.90.86.88", "reason": "applies if the grate is steel/stainless steel (e.g. linear stainless drain covers); material not stated"}, {"code": "3922.90.00", "reason": "applies if the grate is plastic sanitary ware"}, {"code": "7419.20", "reason": "cast copper articles not further worked; shower grates are normally machined/drilled after casting"}, {"code": "7324.90.00", "reason": "sanitary ware of steel covers baths, shower bases and the like, not a drain grate; would only matter if steel"}]}}
{"item_id": "atlas_test_00130", "reference_source": "ruling:K85180", "product": "laminated BOPP/PET film", "cbp_reference": "CBP ruling K85180, gold code 3920.20.0000:\nve and passed through ovens where the coating is dried. It then passes through a laminator where it is bonded with the polypropylene film, which is being unwound at another section of the machine. One silicone coated polyester liner roll will become a part of multiple product rolls. The final rolls of laminate will be shipped back to the United States at 79 inches wide and 15,000 feet long. After reimportation into the United States, the 79 inch wide laminate rolls will be shipped to a finishing center where they will be cut into narrower widths and shorter lengths for eventual use as labels. The applicable subheading for the film will be 3920.20.0000, Harmonized Tariff Schedule of the United States (HTS), which provides for other plates, sheets, film, foil and strip, of plastics, noncellular and not reinforced, laminated, supported or similarly combined with other materials…of polymers of propylene. The rate of duty will be 4.2 percent ad valorem. You have inquired about an exemption of duty for goods that contain components of American origin that have been assembled abroad. Subheading 9802.00.80, HTS, provides for a partial duty exemption for articles assembled abroad in whole or in part of fabricated components, the product of the United States, which (a) were exported in condition ready for assembly without further fabrication, (b) have not lost their physical identity in such articles by change in form, shape or otherwise, and (c) have not been advanced in value or improved in condition abroad except by being assembled and except by operations incidental to the assembly process such as cleaning, lubricating and painting. All three requirements of subheading 9802.00.80, HTS, must be satisfied before a component may receive a duty allowance. An article entered under this tariff provision is subject to duty upon the full cost or value of the imported assembled article, less the cost or value of the U.S. components assembled therein, upon compliance with the documentary requirements of section 10.24, Customs Regulations (19 CFR 10.24). Section 10.14(a), Customs Regulations [19 CFR 10.14(a)], requires that the components must be in condition ready for assembly without further fabrication at the time of their exportation from the United States to qualify for the exemption. Components will not lose their entitlement to the exemption by being subjected to operations incidental to the assembly either before, during, or after their assembly with other components. Section 10.16(a), Customs Regulations [19 CFR 10.16(a)], provides that the assembly operation performed abroad may consist of any method used to join or fit together solid components, such as welding, soldering, riveting, force fitting, gluing, lamination, sewing, or the use of fasteners. Operations incidental to the assembly process are not considered further fabrication operations, as they are of a minor nature and cannot always be provided for in advance of the assembly operations. See 19 CFR 10.16(a). In this case, laminating the United States origin polyester film to the Belgium origin polypropylene film is an acceptable assembly operation pursuant to 19 CFR 10.16(a). Furthermore, cutting the polyester film to length in Germany is enumerated as an acceptable operation incidental to the assembly process under 19 CFR 10.16(b)(6). The assembly meets the three criteria listed above. Therefore, the laminated BOPP/PET film is eligible for a partial duty exemption under subheading 98", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "3920.20.00.55", "deciding_gri": "GRI 6", "gri_path": ["GRI 1: heading 3920 covers plates, sheets, film, foil and strip of plastics, noncellular and not reinforced, laminated, supported or similarly combined with other materials; lamination of one plastic to another plastic stays in 3920", "Headings 3919 (self-adhesive) and 3921 (cellular/reinforced/combined with non-plastics) do not apply on the facts given", "GRI 6 with chapter 39 principles (note 4 / subheading note 1 approach): a multi-polymer film goes to the subheading of the polymer predominating by weight; BOPP assumed to predominate (named first and usually the thicker layer) -> 3920.20 of polymers of propylene; suffix .55 other"], "rationale": "A laminate of BOPP and PET films is a noncellular, unreinforced plastic film of heading 3920. The 6-digit subheading depends on which polymer predominates by weight: polypropylene gives 3920.20 and PET gives 3920.62. The weight split is not stated. Assuming BOPP predominates, the code is 3920.20.00.55, consistent with NY K84976 (in force), which classified multilayer BOPP sheets in 3920.20.0000.", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "3920.62.00.90", "reason": "PET film other than metallized; would apply if the PET layer predominates by weight"}, {"code": "3920.62.00.50", "reason": "metallized PET; no metallization stated"}, {"code": "3921.90", "reason": "only for films that are cellular, reinforced or combined with non-plastic materials; this is a plastic-to-plastic laminate"}, {"code": "3923.21/3923.29", "reason": "the goods are film, not made-up bags or containers"}]}}
{"item_id": "atlas_test_00131", "reference_source": "atlas_reasoning", "product": "pyridine (CAS# 110-86-1) from China", "cbp_reference": "Reasoning summarized from the CBP ruling (gold code 2933.31.0000):\nThe HTS US Code 2933.31.0000 is applicable for pyridine, which is classified under the broader category of \"Heterocyclic compounds with nitrogen hetero-atom(s) only.\" Pyridine is specifically identified as a heterocyclic compound containing a nitrogen atom in its ring structure. The ruling from NY 876120 confirms this classification, indicating that pyridine falls under the subheading for \"pyridine and its derivatives.\" The ruling also states that the rate of duty for this classification is free, which is relevant for importation purposes. Additionally, the ruling provides guidance on the documentation required during the import process, emphasizing the importance of referencing this ruling to Customs officials when filing entry documents.", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "2933.31.00.00", "deciding_gri": "GRI 1", "gri_path": ["GRI 1: chapter 29 note 1(a) covers separate chemically defined organic compounds; pyridine is a heterocyclic compound with nitrogen hetero-atom only, heading 2933", "GRI 6: 2933.3 compounds containing an unfused pyridine ring; 2933.31 names 'Pyridine and its salts' eo nomine; single statistical line 2933.31.00.00"], "rationale": "Pyridine (CAS 110-86-1) is a separate chemically defined heterocyclic compound whose only hetero-atom is nitrogen, so it belongs in heading 2933 under chapter 29 note 1(a). Subheading 2933.31 names pyridine and its salts, which settles the code at GRI 1 and GRI 6. No ruling on pyridine itself came up in the search: the rulings found concern pyridine derivatives in 2933.39, which confirms that only the parent compound goes in 2933.31. Country of origin (China) affects Chapter 99 additional duties, not the classification.", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "2933.39", "reason": "other compounds containing an unfused pyridine ring (pyridine derivatives); pyridine itself is named in 2933.31"}, {"code": "2707.99", "reason": "crude pyridine bases from coal tar distillation; the good is chemically defined pyridine identified by CAS number"}]}}
{"item_id": "atlas_test_00133", "reference_source": "atlas_reasoning", "product": "2,2'-Dithiosalicylic Acid", "cbp_reference": "Reasoning summarized from the CBP ruling (gold code 2930.90.2900):\nThe product in question, 2,2'-Dithiosalicylic Acid (CAS 119-80-2), is classified under the Harmonized Tariff Schedule of the United States (HTSUS) as an organo-sulfur compound. The ruling indicates that it falls under subheading 2930.90.2900, which specifically covers \"organo-sulfur compounds: other.\" The classification is justified because the chemical structure of 2,2'-Dithiosalicylic Acid contains sulfur atoms bonded to carbon, fitting the definition of organo-sulfur compounds. Additionally, the ruling states that this compound is used as a pharmaceutical intermediate, further supporting its classification under the specified HTS code. The applicable duty rate for this classification is 6.7 percent ad valorem, as noted in the ruling.", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "2930.90.29.00", "deciding_gri": "GRI 1", "gri_path": ["GRI 1: chapter 29 note 1(a), separate chemically defined organic compound", "Chapter 29 note 3: prima facie classifiable as an aromatic carboxylic acid (2917) and as an organo-sulfur compound (2930); classify in the heading last in numerical order, 2930", "GRI 6: 2930.90 other organo-sulfur compounds; aromatic; other; other -> 2930.90.29.00"], "rationale": "2,2'-Dithiosalicylic acid is an aromatic disulfide carrying carboxylic acid groups. Under chapter 29 note 3, a compound that fits both the carboxylic acid heading and the organo-sulfur heading goes to the one last in numerical order, 2930. As an aromatic compound not specially named, it falls in 2930.90.29.00. NY N264976 (in force) classified the analogous aromatic disulfide acid 3,3'-dithiobishydrocinnamic acid in 2930.90.2900.", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "2917.39", "reason": "aromatic polycarboxylic acid heading; chapter 29 note 3 sends the compound to the later heading 2930"}, {"code": "2918.29", "reason": "carboxylic acids with additional oxygen function; the extra function is sulfur (disulfide), not oxygen"}, {"code": "2930.90.92", "reason": "nonaromatic organo-sulfur compounds; this compound is aromatic"}]}}
{"item_id": "atlas_test_00135", "reference_source": "atlas_reasoning", "product": "a women's jacket made from polyester pile knit faux fur fabric bonded to leather", "cbp_reference": "Reasoning summarized from the CBP ruling (gold code 6102.30.0500):\nThe product in question is a women's jacket constructed from 100% polyester pile knit faux fur fabric bonded to 100% leather. The ruling indicates that the leather comprises 56% by weight of the garment, while the knit fabric comprises 44%. According to the Harmonized Tariff Schedule (HTS), specifically subheading 6102.30.0500, this classification is applicable for women's overcoats and similar articles that are knitted and made of manmade fibers, provided they contain 25% or more by weight of leather. Since the jacket meets this criterion with 56% leather, it falls under this HTS code. The duty rate for this classification is 5.6% ad valorem, and it is categorized under textile category designation 635. Additionally, as the product is from China, it is subject to quota and visa requirements based on international textile trade agreements.", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "4203.10.40.60", "deciding_gri": "GRI 3(b)", "gri_path": ["GRI 1: chapter 43 note on artificial fur excludes imitation fur obtained by knitting (heading 6001), so heading 4304 does not apply; the faux fur is a knit pile textile", "GRI 1/GRI 2(b)/GRI 3(a): the jacket is prima facie classifiable in 4203 (leather apparel) and 6102 (knit women's coats of man-made fibers); each heading covers only part of the materials, so they are equally specific", "GRI 3(b): composite good; the leather is presumed to give the essential character (usually the larger share of value and weight, as in NY J87560)", "GRI 6: 4203.10.40 other leather apparel; coats and jackets; other; women's -> .60"], "rationale": "A jacket of knit polyester pile faux fur bonded to leather is a composite of a leather-apparel material (4203) and a knit textile (6102). The knitted imitation fur is not artificial fur of 4304. Under GRI 3(b), NY J87560 (in force) found leather gave the essential character of a women's jacket of leather bonded to knit faux sherpa, based on outer surface, weight and value, and classified it in 4203.10.4060. The same result is assumed here, but if the faux fur forms the outer surface and dominates, the jacket would go to 6102.30.20.10.", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "6102.30.20.10", "reason": "women's knit MMF jacket; would apply if the polyester pile faux fur (e.g. as the outer surface) were found to give the essential character"}, {"code": "4304.00.00", "reason": "artificial fur excludes knitted imitation fur of heading 6001"}, {"code": "4203.10.40.95", "reason": "covers women's leather apparel other than coats and jackets"}]}}
{"item_id": "atlas_test_00137", "reference_source": "atlas_reasoning", "product": "unfinished forklifts powered by internal combustion engines using liquid propane gas (LPG)", "cbp_reference": "Reasoning summarized from the CBP ruling (gold code 8427.20.4000):\nThe product in question is classified under HTS code 8427.20.4000, which pertains to \"other self-propelled fork-lift trucks: rider-type, counterbalanced fork-lift trucks.\" The ruling specifies that the forklifts are unfinished, meaning they are imported without essential components such as counterweights, lifting forks, and masts. Despite being unfinished, they are still categorized as fork-lift trucks due to their design and intended use. Furthermore, the forklifts are powered by internal combustion engines that utilize liquid propane gas (LPG), which is consistent with the characteristics of self-propelled fork-lift trucks. The ruling confirms that the applicable subheading for these unfinished forklift trucks is indeed 8427.20.4000, and it also states that the duty rate for this classification is free, reinforcing the appropriateness of this HTS code for the described product.", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "8427.20.40.00", "deciding_gri": "GRI 2(a)", "gri_path": ["GRI 1: heading 8427 covers fork-lift trucks; Section XVI note/Chapter 84 checked, no exclusion", "GRI 2(a): an unfinished forklift that has the essential character of the complete truck (chassis, engine, drivetrain, operator station) is classified as the finished truck", "GRI 6: 8427.20 other self-propelled trucks (not electric motor powered, since propulsion is an LPG internal combustion engine); 8427.20.40 rider-type counterbalanced fork-lift trucks, the standard IC-engine LPG forklift configuration"], "rationale": "Under GRI 2(a), an unfinished forklift that already has its chassis, LPG internal combustion engine and drivetrain has the essential character of a complete fork-lift truck of heading 8427. Because it is propelled by an internal combustion engine rather than an electric motor, it falls in 8427.20. LPG forklifts are normally rider-type counterbalanced, which gives 8427.20.40.00. NY N329057 (in force) shows the parallel treatment of a seated electric forklift in 8427.10.40, the electric-motor counterpart.", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "8427.10", "reason": "self-propelled trucks powered by an electric motor; these are powered by LPG internal combustion engines"}, {"code": "8427.20.80.90", "reason": "other self-propelled works trucks; applies only if the truck is not a rider-type counterbalanced forklift"}, {"code": "8431.20.00", "reason": "parts of 8427 machinery; an unfinished truck with the essential character of the whole is classified as the complete truck under GRI 2(a)"}]}}
{"item_id": "atlas_test_00138", "reference_source": "ruling:H88105", "product": "a Girls Princess Costume Set consisting of a long dress, crown, belt, and necklace made from synthetic fibers and a Hippie Costume consisting of pants, blouse, vest, flower, headband, sunglasses, and necklace with various materials", "cbp_reference": "CBP ruling H88105, gold code 6104.43.2020:\n the article from which the set derives its essential character. In this case, for the Girls Princess Costume, Style GGH1 the dress imparts the essential character of the set. The Hippie Costume, Style GGH3 consists of two garments, a blouse and a skirt. Note 13 of Section XI, of the HTSUSA, requires that the textile garments of different headings be separately classified, thus preventing classification of costumes consisting of two or more garments as sets. If a set cannot exist by application of Note 13, the articles that may be packaged with the garments must also be classified separately. The applicable subheading for the Princess Costume, Style GGH1 will be 6104.43.2020, Harmonized Tariff Schedule of the United States (HTS), which provides for “Women’s or girls’…dresses…knitted or crocheted: Dresses: Of synthetic fibers: Other, Girls’.” The duty rate will be 16.2% ad valorem. The textile category designation is 636. Even though the crown for Style GGH1-02 crown is included as a constituent part of the set for classification purposes, it still falls within textile category 659, and products originating in China are subject to visa and quota requirements which must still be met. The applicable subheading for GGH3--01 the Hippie Costume pants will be 6104.63.2028, Harmonized Tariff Schedule of the United States (HTS), which provides for “Women’s or girls’…trousers…knitted or crocheted…Trousers…Of synthetic fibers: Other: Other, Trousers and breeches: Girls’: Other: Other.” The duty rate will be 28.6% ad valorem. The textile category designation is 648. The applicable subheading for the Hippie Costume blouse GGH3-02 will be 6206.30.3060, Harmonized Tariff Schedule of the United States (HTS), which provides for “Women’s or girls’ blouses, shirts and shirt-blouses: Of cotton: Other: Other, Other.” The duty rate will be 15.6% ad valorem. The textile category designation is 341. The applicable subheading for the Hippie Costume vest GGH3-03 will be 6210.10.9040, Harmonized Tariff Schedule of the United States (HTS), which provides for “Garments, made up of fabrics of heading 5602, 5603, 5903, 5906 or 5907: Of fabrics of heading 5602 or 5603: Other: Other: Other, Other.” The duty rate is 16.2% ad valorem. The textile category designation is 659. The applicable subheading for the Hippie Costume flower GGH3-04 will be 6702.90.3500, Harmonized Tariff Schedule of the United States (HTS), which provides for “Artificial flowers…Of other materials: Other: Of man-made fibers. The duty rate will be 9% ad valorem. The applicable subheading for the Hippie Costume headband GGH3-05 will be 6217.10.8500, Harmonized Tariff Schedule of the United States (HTS), which provides for “Other made up clothing accessories…Accessories: Other: Headbands, ponytail holders and similar articles. The duty rate will be 14.8% ad valorem. The applicable subheading for the Hippie Costume sunglasses, GGH3-06 will be 9004.90.0000, Harmonized Tariff Schedule of the United States (HTS), which provides for “Spectacles, goggles…Other.” The duty rate will be 2.5% ad valorem. The Hippie costume necklace will be classified as follows depending on the value. The applicable subheading for the Hippie Costume necklace, GGH3-07 will be 7117.90.5500, Harmonized Tariff Schedule of the United States (HTS), which provides for “Imitation jewelry: Other: Valued not over 20 cents per dozen pieces or parts: Other.” The duty rate will be 7.2% ad valorem. The applicable subheading for the Hippie Co", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "6104.43.20.20", "deciding_gri": "GRI 3(b)", "gri_path": ["First-named article classified (Princess Costume Set); the Hippie Costume is a separate article to classify separately", "Chapter 95 note 1 excludes textile fancy dress of chapters 61/62 unless flimsy, non-durable festive articles; the dress is presumed a durable, well-made garment, so chapter 61/62 applies", "GRI 3(b): the set's items fall in different headings (dress 6104, crown/headwear, belt, imitation jewelry 7117); the long dress gives the essential character", "GRI 6: 6104.43 knitted dresses of synthetic fibers; 6104.43.20 other; girls' -> .20"], "rationale": "The Princess Costume Set is the first-named article. It is a GRI 3(b) set in which the long synthetic dress gives the essential character over the crown, belt and necklace. A well-made child's costume dress is wearing apparel, not a chapter 95 festive article, so it goes in heading 6104 when knit. NY H80172 (in force) classified a child's Princess Costume dress in 6104.43.2020 (girls' knit synthetic dress). The knit/woven construction and the separate Hippie Costume are open points.", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "6204.43.40", "reason": "girls' woven synthetic dresses; would apply if the dress fabric is woven rather than knit"}, {"code": "9505.90.60.00", "reason": "festive articles/costumes of chapter 95 are limited to flimsy, non-durable costumes; this costume is presumed well made"}, {"code": "6104.43.20.10", "reason": "women's; the costume is for girls"}]}}
{"item_id": "atlas_test_00145", "reference_source": "ruling:N281599", "product": "unisex costumes made from 100% polyester knit fabric for infants and toddlers", "cbp_reference": "CBP ruling N281599, gold code 6111.30.5070:\n on the classification of costumes in its decision in Rubie’s Costume Co. v. United States, slip op 02-1373 (Fed. Cir. Aug. 1, 2003). The decision stated that all flimsy, non-durable textile costumes that are not ordinary articles of apparel are classified under 9505.90.6000 (flimsy); all textile costumes that do not meet flimsy, non-durable standards (well made), or are ordinary articles of apparel are classified in chapters 61 or 62. The overall amount of finishing is such that the articles are neither flimsy in nature or construction, nor lacking in durability; your costumes are well made. The applicable subheading for Item number #F82335-raccoon & monkey costume will be 6111.30.5070, Harmonized Tariff Schedule of the United States (HTSUS), which provides for “Babies’ garments and clothing accessories, knitted or crocheted: Of synthetic fibers: Other: Other: Other.” The rate of duty will be 16 percent ad valorem. The applicable subheading for Item number #F82266-cupcake costume (Infant) will be 6111.30.5020, HTSUS, which provides for \"Babies’ garments and clothing accessories, knitted or crocheted, Of synthetic fibers: Other, Sets.\" The duty rate will be 16 percent ad valorem. The applicable subheading for Item number #F82271-dog costume (Infant) will be 6111.30.4000, HTSUS, which provides for “Babies’ garments and clothing accessories, knitted or crocheted: Of synthetic fibers: Sweaters, pullovers, sweatshirts, waistcoats (vests) and similar articles, except those imported as parts of sets.” The rate of duty will be 30 percent ad valorem. The applicable subheading for Item number #F82335-raccoon & monkey costume (Toddler) will be 6102.30.2020, HTSUS, which provides for Women’s or girls’ overcoats, carcoats, capes, cloaks, anoraks (including ski-jackets), windbreakers and similar articles, knitted or crocheted, other than those of heading 6104: Of man-made fibers: Other: Other: Girls’. The rate of duty will be 28.2 percent ad valorem. The applicable subheading for Item number #F82266-cupcake costume (Toddler) consist(s) of two or more garments. However, Note 14 of Section XI, of the HTSUS, requires that textile garments of different headings be separately classified, thus preventing classification of costumes consisting of two or more garments as sets. If a set cannot exist by application of Note 14, the articles that may be packaged with the garments must also be classified separately. The applicable subheading for Item numbers #F82266-cupcake costume (Toddler) and #F82271-dog costume (Toddler) pullover will be 6110.30.3059, HTSUS, which provides for Sweaters, pullovers, sweatshirts, waistcoats (vests) and similar articles, knitted or crocheted (con.): Of man-made fibers: Other: Other: Other: Women’s or girls’: Other. The rate of duty will be 32 percent ad valorem. The applicable subheading for Item number #F82266-cupcake costume (Toddler) pull-on pants will be 6104.63.2028, HTSUS, which provides for Women’s or girls’ suits, ensembles, suit-type jackets, blazers, dresses, skirts, divided skirts, trousers, bib and brace overalls, breeches and shorts (other than swimwear), knitted or crocheted: trousers, bib and brace overalls, breeches and shorts: of synthetic fibers: other: other, trousers and breeches: girls’: other: other. The rate of duty will be 28.2 percent ad valorem. Duty rates are provided for your convenience and are subject to change. The text of the most recent HTSUS and the accompanying duty rates are provided on World W", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "6111.30.50.20", "deciding_gri": "GRI 1", "gri_path": ["Chapter 95 note 1 / Rubie's Costume: only flimsy, non-durable textile costumes go to 9505.90.60; well-made costumes are wearing apparel of chapter 61", "GRI 1: chapter 61 note defines babies' garments (young children up to 86 cm), so heading 6111 applies to the infant sizes", "GRI 3(b): costume of jumpsuit plus headpiece put up together; the jumpsuit gives the essential character", "GRI 6: 6111.30 of synthetic fibers; 6111.30.50 other; statistical suffix .20 sets"], "rationale": "Well-made knit polyester costumes are wearing apparel rather than festive articles of 9505, which covers only flimsy, non-durable costumes. For infant sizes, heading 6111 (babies' garments, knitted) applies, in 6111.30.50 as synthetic fiber garments. A jumpsuit with headpiece is reported as a set, suffix .20. NY N107290 (in force) classified an infant knit polyester costume of jumpsuit and hat in 6111.30.5020 and a toddler-size costume in 6114.30.", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "9505.90.60.00", "reason": "only for flimsy, non-durable costumes; these are presumed of ordinary garment construction"}, {"code": "6114.30.30", "reason": "other knit MMF garments; applies to toddler sizes above 86 cm body height (classify those sizes separately)"}, {"code": "6111.30.50.70", "reason": "other babies' garments; applies if the costume is a single garment not put up as a set"}]}}
{"item_id": "atlas_test_00146", "reference_source": "atlas_reasoning", "product": "a toilet set containing goldplated implements including tweezers, nail clippers, safety scissors, a pen knife, and a shoe horn, all housed in a leather zip top case", "cbp_reference": "Reasoning summarized from the CBP ruling (gold code 8205.60.0000):\nThe product in question is a toilet set that includes various grooming implements. Each implement is classified separately due to the inclusion of items that do not belong to the same category, specifically the shoe horn and pen knife. The classification is as follows: \n1. The leather zip top case itself is classified under HTS 9603.90.8050 as a brush (other) because it functions as a holder for the implements.\n2. The tweezers are classified under HTS 8203.20.2000, which specifically covers tweezers.\n3. The nail clippers fall under HTS 8214.20.3000, which is for clippers.\n4. The safety scissors are classified under HTS 8213.00.9000 as other scissors.\n5. The pen knife is classified under HTS 8211.93.0030, which covers knives with folding blades.\n6. Finally, the shoe horn is classified under HTS 8205.59.8000, which is for hand tools classified as other.\n\nSince the question specifically asks for the toilet set as a whole, the classification of the shoe horn under HTS 8205.59.8000 is relevant, as it is included in the overall product description, leading to the classification of the entire set under HTS 8205.60.0000, which encompasses hand tools and implements.", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "9605.00.00.00", "deciding_gri": "GRI 1", "gri_path": ["GRI 1: heading 9605 covers travel sets for personal toilet, sewing or shoe or clothes cleaning, other than manicure and pedicure sets of 8214", "The set is not limited to manicure/pedicure instruments (it includes a pen knife and a shoe horn), so it is not a manicure set of 8214.20 and falls in 9605 eo nomine", "GRI 5(a): the fitted leather case is classified with the set", "GRI 6: 9605.00.00.00 single statistical line"], "rationale": "The article is a toilet set in a fitted leather case. Its implements go beyond manicure and pedicure instruments (a shoe horn and a pen knife are included), so it is a travel set for personal toilet of heading 9605 rather than a manicure set of 8214. NY H83408 (in force) classified a leather-cased travel grooming set containing a shoe horn and nail accessories in 9605.00.0000, and a pure manicure set in 8214.20.6000. The leather case follows the set under GRI 5(a).", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "8214.20.60.00", "reason": "manicure or pedicure sets in leather containers; the set also contains a shoe horn and pen knife, so it is more than a manicure set"}, {"code": "4202.31", "reason": "the leather case is a GRI 5(a) container classified with its contents"}, {"code": "8211.93", "reason": "pen knife alone; the set is classified as a whole under heading 9605"}]}}
{"item_id": "atlas_test_00147", "reference_source": "atlas_reasoning", "product": "a flexible circuit assembly consisting of a flexible circuit substrate with copper runs, a polyimide insulating coating, gold edge fingers for connection, and two non-printed connectors", "cbp_reference": "Reasoning summarized from the CBP ruling (gold code 8534.00.0080):\nThe product in question is a flexible circuit assembly (PN-190896) that includes a flexible circuit substrate with copper traces and a polyimide insulating coating. The copper traces are created through an etching process, which classifies the item as a printed circuit. According to the Harmonized Tariff Schedule (HTSUS), printed circuits are classified under heading 8534. The Explanatory Notes (ENs) for heading 8534 indicate that printed circuits may include non-printed connecting elements, which is applicable in this case as the assembly features two non-printed connectors. These connectors are intended for future connections to mechanical or electrical components, but at the time of importation, they do not include such components. Therefore, the assembly is not excluded from heading 8534 classification. The specific subheading for this flexible circuit assembly is 8534.00.0080, which covers printed circuits of the flexible type. Additionally, products classified under this subheading that are imported from China are subject to an additional 10 percent ad valorem duty, as noted in the ruling, requiring the reporting of the relevant Chapter 99 subheading (9903.88.03) alongside the primary HTS code.", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "8534.00.00.80", "deciding_gri": "GRI 1", "gri_path": ["GRI 1: chapter 85 note 8 defines printed circuits of heading 8534 as conductor patterns formed on an insulating base by a printing process, and expressly allows them to be fitted with non-printed connecting elements", "The two connectors and gold edge fingers are connecting elements, so the assembly remains a printed circuit and does not become a printed circuit assembly of 8473/8517/8538/8543 etc.", "Section XVI note 2(a): a part that is a good of heading 8534 is classified in its own heading", "GRI 6: 8534.00.00 printed circuits; other; flexible type -> .80"], "rationale": "Chapter 85 note 8 defines printed circuits as conductor patterns formed on an insulating base, and states that they may be fitted with non-printed connecting elements. A flexible substrate with copper runs, polyimide coating, gold edge fingers and two connectors therefore stays in heading 8534. Under Section XVI note 2(a), the parts provisions do not apply. The flexible type line is 8534.00.00.80. NY N325811 (in force) confirms that heading 8534 takes precedence over parts provisions for printed circuit substrates.", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "8536.69.40", "reason": "the connectors are only fitted to the printed circuit; the article as a whole is the printed circuit under chapter 85 note 8"}, {"code": "8542", "reason": "no active elements that produce, rectify, modulate or amplify signals"}, {"code": "8538.90 / 8473.30", "reason": "parts provisions do not apply because 8534 describes the good (Section XVI note 2(a))"}, {"code": "8534.00.00.95", "reason": "other rigid circuits; this circuit is of the flexible type"}]}}
{"item_id": "atlas_test_00154", "reference_source": "atlas_reasoning", "product": "glass filters used in photography", "cbp_reference": "Reasoning summarized from the CBP ruling (gold code 9002.20.4000):\nThe product in question consists of glass filters designed for use with cameras, specifically a circular polarizing filter and a square filter known as the Big Stopper. The circular polarizing filter (item PLC105) is mounted in a ring and is classified under HTSUS 9002.20.4000, which covers \"Lenses, prisms, mirrors and other optical elements, of any material mounted, being parts of or fitting for instruments or apparatus, other than such elements of glass not optically worked; parts and accessories thereof: Filters and parts and accessories thereof: Photographic.\" This classification is appropriate as the filter is specifically designed for photographic use and is mounted for easy attachment to camera equipment. The Big Stopper filter, while also a filter, is classified under a different HTSUS code (9001.90.9000) because it is unmounted and serves a different function. The ruling indicates that the circular polarizing filter meets the criteria for classification under 9002.20.4000 due to its optical properties and intended use in photography.", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "9001.90.90.00", "deciding_gri": "GRI 1", "gri_path": ["GRI 1: optically worked glass filters are optical elements of chapter 90; chapter 70 applies only to glass elements not optically worked (chapter 90 note 1 / heading 9001 text)", "Heading 9001 covers optical elements unmounted; heading 9002 covers them mounted, as parts or fittings of instruments. Assumed unmounted", "GRI 6: 9001.90 other; not lenses, prisms, mirrors or halftone screens -> 9001.90.90.00"], "rationale": "Glass photographic filters are optically worked optical elements of chapter 90. The heading depends on whether they are mounted: unmounted filters fall in 9001.90.90.00 and mounted photographic filters in 9002.20.40.00. NY H81010 (in force) classified unmounted glass photographic optical filters in 9001.90.9000, so that code is the best guess here. Mounting must be confirmed.", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "9002.20.40.00", "reason": "mounted photographic filters; would apply if the filters are mounted in rings/frames for fitting to cameras or lenses"}, {"code": "7014.00", "reason": "optical glass elements not optically worked; photographic filters are optically worked"}, {"code": "9001.90.80.00", "reason": "halftone screens for engraving or photographic processes are a different article"}]}}
{"item_id": "atlas_test_00156", "reference_source": "atlas_reasoning", "product": "a ship propulsion unit", "cbp_reference": "Reasoning summarized from the CBP ruling (gold code 8431.49.1090):\nThe product in question is a ship propulsion unit, which falls under the broader category of \"parts suitable for use solely or principally with the machines of headings 8425 to 8430\" as specified in HTSUS subheading 8431. The specific subheading 8431.49.1090 pertains to parts of marine propulsion systems. Initially, the product was entered under subheading 9808.00.30, which provides duty-free treatment for emergency war materials purchased by U.S. military departments. However, since the required certification for this duty-free treatment was not provided at the time of entry, Customs liquidated the entry under subheading 8431.49.1090, which is applicable to the ship propulsion unit. The ruling confirms that the merchandise is indeed eligible for classification under this HTSUS code, as it aligns with the description of the product and the applicable regulations.", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "8408.10.00.50", "deciding_gri": "GRI 1", "gri_path": ["GRI 1: candidate headings are 8408.10 (marine propulsion diesel engines), 8407.21/.29 (spark-ignition marine engines), 8483.40 (gearing/drive units, e.g. stern or Z-drives), 8501 (electric propulsion motors) and 8487.10 (ship propellers)", "Section XVI note 3: a composite unit (engine + reduction gear + shaft/propeller) is classified by its principal function, the power source supplying propulsion -> marine propulsion engine; assumed diesel", "GRI 6: 8408.10.00 marine propulsion engines; ship-scale output assumed to exceed 746 kW -> .50"], "rationale": "The description gives only 'a ship propulsion unit', so the heading cannot be fixed. A unit that incorporates its own power plant takes the principal function of propulsion under Section XVI note 3 and would be a marine propulsion engine of 8408.10, most likely above 746 kW for a ship. A drive unit without an engine would instead go to 8483.40 (as the outboard drive in NY H82350, in force), and an electric pod to 8501. The unit's composition and power source must be confirmed.", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "8483.40.90.00", "reason": "applies if the unit is a drive/transmission (stern drive, Z-drive, azimuth thruster gear unit) without its own engine, as for the outboard drive in H82350"}, {"code": "8501.53", "reason": "applies if the unit is an electric propulsion motor/pod"}, {"code": "8487.10.00", "reason": "applies only to ship propellers and blades imported alone"}, {"code": "8407.29.00", "reason": "spark-ignition marine engines; ship propulsion is normally diesel"}, {"code": "8408.10.00.40", "reason": "power 373-746 kW; ship-scale output assumed higher"}]}}
{"item_id": "atlas_test_00159", "reference_source": "ruling:I89291", "product": "men's knit pullover garments made from hemp and cotton", "cbp_reference": "CBP ruling I89291, gold code 6110.90.9088:\nric that measures 20 stitches per two centimeters counted in the horizontal direction. Style MWRU2553 features a rib knit crew neckline with a cross over V at the center front, short sleeves with rib knit cuffs, and a rib knit bottom. Style MWRU2791 is a men’s garment constructed from 54 percent silk, 26 percent rayon, 20 percent viscose knit fabric that measures 15 stitches per two centimeters counted in the horizontal direction. Style MWRU2791 features a rib knit spread collar, a full front opening with seven button closures, short sleeves with tubular knit cuffs, and a tubular knit bottom. The applicable subheading for Styles MWRU2553 and MWRU2791 will be 6110.90.9088, Harmonized Tariff Schedule of the United States, (HTS), which provides for: sweaters, pullovers, sweatshirts, waistcoats (vests) and similar articles, knitted or crocheted: of other textiles materials: other: other: other: men's or boys'. The rate of duty is 6 percent ad valorem. In 2003, the rate of duty will be the same. Styles MWRU2553 and MWRU2791 fall within textile category designation 838. Based upon international textile trade agreements, products of China are not presently subject to visa requirements or quota restraints. The designated textile and apparel categories and their quota and visa status are the result of international agreements that are subject to frequent renegotiations and changes. To obtain the most current information, we suggest that you check, close to the time of shipment, the U.S. Customs Service Textile Status Report, an internal issuance of the U.S. Customs Service, which is available at the Customs Web site at www.customs.gov. In addition, the designated textile and apparel categories may be subdivided into parts. If so, visa and quota requirements applicable to the subject merchandise may be affected and should also be verified at the time of shipment. This ruling is being issued under the provisions of Part 177 of the Customs Regulations (19 C.F.R. 177). A copy of this ruling letter or the control number indicated above should be provided with the entry documents filed at the time this merchandise is imported. If you have any questions regarding this ruling, contact National Import Specialist Mary Ryan at 646-733-3271. Sincerely, Robert B. Swierupski Director, National Commodity Specialist Division", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "6110.90.90.88", "deciding_gri": "GRI 6", "gri_path": ["GRI 1: heading 6110 covers knitted sweaters, pullovers and similar articles; chapter 61 note 1 (made-up knit articles) satisfied", "Section XI subheading note 2(A)/GRI 3(b) at subheading level: classified by the fiber predominating by weight; assumed hemp (other vegetable fiber) predominates, so 6110.90 'of other textile materials' rather than 6110.20 'of cotton'", "GRI 6: 6110.90.90 other (not 70% silk); statistical suffix: 'Other' pullover (not sweater/vest), not subject to cotton/wool/MMF restraints (cotton under 50%), men's or boys' (838)"], "rationale": "A men's knitted pullover falls in heading 6110 by GRI 1. The subheading depends on the chief-weight fiber: hemp is an 'other textile material', so if hemp predominates the garment goes to 6110.90.90, and with cotton under 50% it is not subject to cotton restraints, giving the men's 'other' line 6110.90.90.88 (838). NY J89828 (in force) placed a men's silk/cotton knit pullover of other textile material in 6110.90.9088, supporting the suffix. Because fiber percentages are not given, the code is a best guess.", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "6110.20.20", "reason": "applies only if cotton predominates by weight over hemp"}, {"code": "6110.90.90.10", "reason": "sweater line for men subject to cotton restraints; garment described as a pullover, not a sweater, and assumed not over 50% cotton"}, {"code": "6110.90.90.67", "reason": "cotton-restraint line applies only when restraint fibers make up 50% or more of weight"}]}}
{"item_id": "atlas_test_00163", "reference_source": "atlas_reasoning", "product": "an integrated valve actuator, an APECS 500 Single Speed Electronic Engine Controller, and APECS digital controllers used in industrial engine control systems", "cbp_reference": "Reasoning summarized from the CBP ruling (gold code 8482.99.2560):\nThe HTS US Code for the integrated valve actuator is classified under subheading 8481.80.9025, which covers other valves with electrical or electro-hydraulic actuators. The integrated valve actuator is designed for throttling the intake of internal combustion engines, which aligns with the description of valves under this subheading. The APECS 500 Single Speed Electronic Engine Controller is classified under subheading 9032.89.6085, which pertains to other automatic regulating or controlling instruments and apparatus, as it sends electrical instructions to maintain engine RPM. Lastly, the APECS DPG-21XX-00X, DPG-2201-00X, and DPG-2223-00X Digital Controllers are classified under subheading 8537.10.9060, which includes programmable controllers for electric control and distribution. These classifications are based on the functionality of the products and their components, as well as their electrical characteristics, which are essential for the operation of industrial engine control systems.", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "9032.89.60.85", "deciding_gri": "GRI 1", "gri_path": ["GRI 1: first-named article (integrated valve actuator) classified; chapter 90 note 7(b) covers automatic regulators of non-electrical quantities (engine speed) that measure the actual value and bring it to and maintain it at a set value", "Section XVI note 1(m) excludes chapter 90 articles, so 8501 (motor) and 8537 (control panels) and 8481 (valves) yield to heading 9032 for a self-contained regulating unit", "GRI 6: 9032.89 other instruments, 9032.89.60 other, statistical suffix .85 other (not a hydraulic/pneumatic or complete climate system line)"], "rationale": "The description names several articles; the first-named integrated valve actuator is classified. An engine speed governing device that compares actual speed to a set point and moves the fuel valve to correct it is an automatic regulator within chapter 90 note 7, and NY K89109 (in force) classified Woodward electrically powered governor controllers and complete governor systems in 9032.89.6085. If the actuator is only a motor with no control function, K89109 shows it would fall in 8501.31, so the decisive fact is listed as missing.", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "8501.31", "reason": "K89109 placed a stand-alone DC-motor actuator with no control function here; an integrated actuator containing its own speed control acts as a regulator of heading 9032"}, {"code": "8537.10.91", "reason": "engine speed controllers that measure and maintain speed are automatic regulators under chapter 90 note 7; Section XVI note 1(m) excludes chapter 90 goods"}, {"code": "8481.80", "reason": "the article is not merely a valve; its function is automatic regulation of engine speed"}]}}
